# Git workflow

## Branches

- Long-lived: `master` (production), `dev` (integration).
- Work only on topic branches. Never commit directly to `master` or `dev`.
- Name: `feature/<slug>`, `bugfix/<slug>`, `hotfix/<slug>`, `chore/<slug>`.
- Base `feature/*` and `bugfix/*` on `dev`. Base `hotfix/*` on `master`, then merge or cherry-pick into `dev`.
- Merge topic branches into `dev`. Merge `dev` into `master` only for a release.

## Atomic commits

- One logical change per commit (one concern: feature slice, bugfix, chore, or docs).
- Do not mix unrelated files or follow-up refactors into the same commit.
- Commit only when the user asks. Message: why, not what; match recent `git log` style.

## Version tags

- Tag only after the adversarial release-gate review (see `adversarial-release-review` rule).
- `master`: annotated SemVer **release** `X.Y.Z` (GitHub Release, not pre-release). Example: `0.0.6`.
- `dev`: annotated SemVer **pre-release** `X.Y.Z-<pre>.N` (GitHub Release with `--prerelease`). Example: `0.0.6-rc.1`.
- Tag the commit on that branch; do not move tags. Do not tag topic branches.
- Existing style has no `v` prefix (`0.0.5-stable`); keep that unless the user asks to change it.
